It is as much like the real world as the skills/preferences of the programmers 
and/or the limits of the hardware allow it to be. Sorry to be "heretical" 
here, Dave, but I think Marty has raised some good points. 

>Marty:
> Maybe the message needs to be spit out into "sated" and "gorged".

No, I don't think it's necessary to add another category. If we were going to
do anything like that, why not just go all the way and list -percentages- of 
"gorging" or "starvation"?

I would be interested to hear from someone with a background in nutrition/
medicine/physiology/biology, however. It seems to me that, in real life, it 
would be next to IMPOSSIBLE for someone to kill themselves by over-feeding, 
even if it was voluntary. Wouldn't they just get slower, and SLOWER, and 
S-L-O-W-E-R and finally they would just stop and/or pass out? 

In NetHack terms, one could calculate food-consumption rates with a much finer 
grain than currently, and allow for much slower rates at high satiation 
levels. Thus, at a certain point, the rate of nutrition would achieve parity 
with the normal rate of metabolism, i.e. continued eating would not derive 
-any- marginal nutritional benefit at all. Choking would be impossible, except 
maybe as a random chance that, while ramming the food down, some piece gets 
lodged in the player's esophagus.

For balance, I would then prefer to eliminate the player's control over how 
long to continue eating a given meal (the "Stop eating?" prompt). That way, 
eating a large quantity of food while satiated would always take a -long- 
time, and the player would a) effectively -waste- most of the food and b) run 
the risk of having monsters show up and get their first blows in before the 
player had time to prepare.

